#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b563777-4f8c-5979-a3c2-b524ca1810c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}^{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}(x^{2} - 2 x y - 2 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723824} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3ca17f-96b2-5c92-b6ab-280fea6c7485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}^{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}(2 x y - y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723829} {:answer/id #uuid "3d18edcb-a812-5d8c-88ec-2b55cb33043b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{3}(- 2 x y - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723834} {:answer/id #uuid "86112699-ae00-53db-9d72-b7a3d8427ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}^{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}(2 x y - y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723839} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a0c719-dbf0-545b-9297-cacf0f1485b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}^{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}(- 2 x y - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a0c719-dbf0-545b-9297-cacf0f1485b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}^{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}(- 2 x y - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723847} :answer-field/id #uuid "32dc4a3c-8d93-5676-bdf2-ca82c77161b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6816e318-77c2-5e3f-a702-4ed17f6abe92" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $x=y^{2} + 2 y + 1$ and $x=- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^{2} y - x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} - y^{3}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=2 x y - y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- 4 x y - 3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(2 x y - y^{2})+(- 4 x y - 3 y^{2})\\\\ &=- 2 x y - 4 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2} + 2 y + 1&=- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1\\\\ 3\\left(y + 1\\right)^2&=3\\\\ y&=-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $x=y^{2} + 2 y + 1$ and the upper boundary is $x=- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2&\\leq y\\leq 0,\\\\ y^{2} + 2 y + 1&\\leq x\\leq - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/37/3752f342d80decb31e6020ab992998b9090b90396a8b0dac97ca5d3afb132ddc.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}^{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}(- 2 x y - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194f514c-3a7f-5969-9942-fdf6cfa29cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{8 - x^{2}}(- 6 x y + 5 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723826} {:answer/id #uuid "29366996-afb6-5f83-a6fa-d3cb1ab51054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{8 - x^{2}}^{x^{2}}(- 6 x y + 5 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723828} {:answer/id #uuid "383ae6c1-a186-58ff-be4d-8436a831559d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{8 - x^{2}}(- 4 x y + 3 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723833} {:answer/id #uuid "4361ff3d-3de0-5c6d-834d-22c089d3c5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{8 - x^{2}}(8 x^{2} y^{2} - 14 x y^{3} + 6 y^{4})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723835} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0f46e0-4bca-5887-b5ed-c5db4c5f49fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{0}^{8}(8 x^{2} y^{2} - 14 x y^{3} + 6 y^{4})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "194f514c-3a7f-5969-9942-fdf6cfa29cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{8 - x^{2}}(- 6 x y + 5 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723826} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5a43299-b83f-51c8-adc8-4c732269bef0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "95a83238-b47c-5aeb-9bcb-b71cfc208ebb" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $y=x^{2}$ and $y=8 - x^{2}$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} + y^{3}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 2 x y + 2 y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- 4 x y + 3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 2 x y + 2 y^{2})+(- 4 x y + 3 y^{2})\\\\ &=- 6 x y + 5 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}&=8 - x^{2}\\\\ 2\\left(x\\right)^2&=8\\\\ x&=-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $y=x^{2}$ and the upper boundary is $y=8 - x^{2}$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2&\\leq x\\leq 2,\\\\ x^{2}&\\leq y\\leq 8 - x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/87/87f9487571eb2b33b122f986c434756500d25d2d11be5d81d13d808ab15cfeb9.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{8 - x^{2}}(- 6 x y + 5 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8cdb37-3a8a-5226-8be7-f049e039fb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{2 - y^{2}}^{y^{2}}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x - 2 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723825} {:answer/id #uuid "23c1e1ef-76a2-5641-9d52-f22d00b42c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{2 - y^{2}}(- y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723827} {:answer/id #uuid "e769d773-b026-50e2-a830-46cff457e96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{2 - y^{2}}(0)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723848} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1cdc90-d002-5703-9635-62802da6ba7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2}(0)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723850} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7f9b3f-67e9-5267-b608-9b7530ce65e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{2 - y^{2}}(- 2 x^{2} + 2 x - 2 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e769d773-b026-50e2-a830-46cff457e96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{2 - y^{2}}(0)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723848} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eb977d6-f0f4-5ff1-8f78-fa83d8d0a302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d968fb07-7776-523b-aaa1-6f3a55542b8f" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $x=y^{2}$ and $x=2 - y^{2}$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y,\\ - 2 x y^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=4 x y - 2 y\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- 4 x y + 2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(4 x y - 2 y)+(- 4 x y + 2 y)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2}&=2 - y^{2}\\\\ 2\\left(y\\right)^2&=2\\\\ y&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $x=y^{2}$ and the upper boundary is $x=2 - y^{2}$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1&\\leq y\\leq 1,\\\\ y^{2}&\\leq x\\leq 2 - y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/c9/c962eee590078fb59fda8760e0892554dad8cb02da5a360054730717797f9065.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{2 - y^{2}}(0)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b9a8c4-b88e-5239-9aef-54347f215921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724053} {:answer/id #uuid "1c46e596-7042-5b8d-94e8-a7589e01d9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724056} {:answer/id #uuid "1f29a51c-625b-54d0-8faa-aa89a87ed926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724058} {:answer/id #uuid "92e252c0-6532-57d9-98a5-0815e28e26e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724069} {:answer/id #uuid "be82014d-b7ce-552e-bb87-e0a3ff04d6ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c46e596-7042-5b8d-94e8-a7589e01d9a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724056} :answer-field/id #uuid "93590df8-cc3c-5d99-9cdf-7c5acf65fa95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0286bd6b-d501-5211-a24f-98824152eb1e" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - x + e^{y},\\ y^{2} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-2\\le x\\le 1$, $0\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\ 0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -2, 1 and y bounds 0, 1](images/09/090c7ca9a0a029a46dd3a2d324cabd3632574173e85baf6b464f987e3a86e697.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=-1,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2 y - 1.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left(2 y - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[2 x y - x\\right]_{x=-2}^{x=1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(6 y - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[3 y^{2} - 3 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "176ac27e-1d81-5102-bb3f-9d4dc2d05aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724054} {:answer/id #uuid "5da43d9b-9aac-5d92-be5d-a1bd44ea52f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724062} {:answer/id #uuid "784b1e0e-88eb-537c-b736-df8a9c1d23e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724064} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96beb2-d06c-50bc-b839-5a723dff6764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724073} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ed665f-09df-5620-ac62-d2f3c6dd72b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ed665f-09df-5620-ac62-d2f3c6dd72b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724077} :answer-field/id #uuid "71d977ac-fa86-59cd-be31-daeab25b3d97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92d72416-3b27-59f6-8a87-dd26b721b1c2" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\ - 2 y + e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $1\\le x\\le 2$, $-1\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 2,\\ -1\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds 1, 2 and y bounds -1, 1](images/5a/5a7482c055aec608ccc33a59139a65384c3a3f2aaebb15ebbf03c7ac785e59b2.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=4 x,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4 x - 2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(4 x - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left[2 x^{2} - 2 x\\right]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[4 y\\right]_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a341844-fba8-5f44-8205-320eff57f8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724052} {:answer/id #uuid "388f7bec-fdfc-5f61-8efb-9fdcbf77d23a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724059} {:answer/id #uuid "8bddc7b5-8cf2-5502-ad90-411b10b029b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724067} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e07436-9119-5c0a-8910-1af207b30612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724071} {:answer/id #uuid "b8211e05-4e69-5c53-b0b0-c9c4e93d532d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e07436-9119-5c0a-8910-1af207b30612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724071} :answer-field/id #uuid "182e4ea6-f1d5-50bf-b281-bc880f3930a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae57e1d6-a81d-5d62-af52-2cf9ed4e469d" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\ 2 y^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 2$, $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\ 0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 2 and y bounds 0, 2](images/30/306187a2ff03bdf4b7f3c52090b8b5b353902213d297292f200e839df7fea8e4.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=1,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=4 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4 y + 1.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(4 y + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[4 x y + x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(12 y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[6 y^{2} + 3 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(30\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=30.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b5f44a6-d1f0-50e7-b887-6885efd43716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724061} {:answer/id #uuid "8b125d94-0542-5864-82f2-b13516efafcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724066} {:answer/id #uuid "b33d2b5e-05ae-5553-b201-3801ff1ddc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724075} {:answer/id #uuid "b51696d5-56c7-5eea-a561-a5400ca24d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724076} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ca128c-526e-5524-a188-279293140d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9ca128c-526e-5524-a188-279293140d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724081} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da2ede9-62ed-52c3-a2f3-3e3e4b0db5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3c5a318-9226-54b9-b321-59dacfef840f" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + e^{y},\\ - 2 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 0$, $-1\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\ -1\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 0 and y bounds -1, 2](images/4c/4cb4ba6d860090403990851d7c2744e25f477a3f3569e9ef8194b7e3dc389137.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=4 x,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4 x - 2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left(4 x - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left[2 x^{2} - 2 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(-4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 y\\right]_{y=-1}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(-8\\right)-\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f0c6d86-5876-50fa-bb35-c2133ca5509d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724060} {:answer/id #uuid "7f43cbea-f4a6-5881-ae09-4db130ec6e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724065} {:answer/id #uuid "aba3700a-7b6f-5024-b903-84e24cdd8c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{57}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724072} {:answer/id #uuid "e0909745-aa68-5d4c-b193-b8b7bbbad2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{57}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724082} {:answer/id #uuid "f728e106-ac21-544b-8692-7ecdd70ed0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{163}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba3700a-7b6f-5024-b903-84e24cdd8c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{57}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724072} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4aa3fed-27c5-54ca-b9f2-6ca19ce645a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e698dd84-eedd-5c1b-a17a-fa910e325f29" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x y^{3} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\ y^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $0\\le x\\le 2$, $1\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\ 1\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds 0, 2 and y bounds 1, 2](images/9c/9c9655873e8faf6bbad28c0711dc8735d37040e10ac780e5b422414331eed7a6.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=3 y^{3},\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3 y^{3} + 2 y.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(3 y^{3} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left[3 x y^{3} + 2 x y\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(6 y^{3} + 4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 y^{4}}{2} + 2 y^{2}\\right]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(32\\right)-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{57}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d155cb-98d5-548f-ae8e-390f60616784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724126} {:answer/id #uuid "6267c770-e102-5995-9617-c5e5bb2f52d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724131} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3bb7b7-6050-57ed-aef5-a6b0d5969582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724137} {:answer/id #uuid "c30ec0b6-5ad1-5ef4-8e93-58ddb2838eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724139} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ec9ce-8a31-5e27-849e-ac81ee8f3467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc0ec9ce-8a31-5e27-849e-ac81ee8f3467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724150} :answer-field/id #uuid "59044c96-65fb-5131-bddb-0f8a341db1f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1623105-e8ca-55fa-bd78-33bbfecda2f3" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\ - y + e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 2$, $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\ 0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 2 and y bounds 0, 2](images/30/306187a2ff03bdf4b7f3c52090b8b5b353902213d297292f200e839df7fea8e4.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=3,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[2 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[6 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(12\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3831dbda-e10f-5b15-9e5a-69c11c61ebc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724123} {:answer/id #uuid "5a031b81-2ef8-5e6a-8172-1ade2a4fb140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724128} {:answer/id #uuid "5beaebbd-c868-5d8b-92e1-c9dbc163e7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724129} {:answer/id #uuid "6a8cc09b-89a9-5fdf-8933-50dcb40b9760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724133} {:answer/id #uuid "ded09422-a21b-59b1-822d-0da49279d60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a031b81-2ef8-5e6a-8172-1ade2a4fb140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724128} :answer-field/id #uuid "42cedcdb-7e98-5d13-8b86-0bcca4dd8f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e224d805-37c6-5cd5-830e-4e982ccf22ab" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 3 x + e^{y},\\ y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-2\\le x\\le 0$, $-2\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 0,\\ -2\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -2, 0 and y bounds -2, 1](images/ce/ce4b4868957375d8b936fe85217dda7c29f782af68464900113b0cd374cf2715.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=-3,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=-2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\int_{-2}^{0}\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left[- 2 x\\right]_{x=-2}^{x=0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left(-4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 y\\right]_{y=-2}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-4\\right)-\\left(8\\right)\\\\\n&=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d12e373-c7ca-5319-9e53-ead7637ab5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724130} {:answer/id #uuid "84c213d4-1dd8-5d9f-a649-4943c85c9c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724135} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc60c39-347d-5db8-b184-8000f2699132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724138} {:answer/id #uuid "c405d0ce-b7a6-5174-9df2-1dd4313bed21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724140} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ac72ac-d0c7-5a1c-ab16-5f9e975e63a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc60c39-347d-5db8-b184-8000f2699132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724138} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb5a92b-e9cf-5f2c-9aba-1bcab6e57be9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed6f61f6-d258-5ed4-9aaf-751d2e84d059" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 2 x + e^{y},\\ - 3 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 2$, $-1\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\ -1\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 2 and y bounds -1, 1](images/e9/e95eba59b4da9ca3035998f82c261a8a64e09c46ed68178e4789372e45f3deb9.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=-2,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=-5.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(-5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left[- 5 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(-15\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[- 15 y\\right]_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-15\\right)-\\left(15\\right)\\\\\n&=-30.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724147 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e9b5b9-33c1-5f5a-b059-d8bd19727bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724132} {:answer/id #uuid "9652cb34-0fe5-5913-be08-7c26c8bcc1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724136} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bf7554-24f0-58b9-99ec-576b8b58778d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724141} {:answer/id #uuid "f4090c18-1caa-5214-848e-85840173a461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724148} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d3a20b-6178-5679-8492-ba8fa7c34943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65e9b5b9-33c1-5f5a-b059-d8bd19727bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724132} :answer-field/id #uuid "400bab3a-baf2-5424-87b2-d86bfd9b8fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f400a85e-b7e8-5eda-a9f1-55bbe661e4be" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\ 3 y + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 1$, $0\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\ 0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 1 and y bounds 0, 1](images/07/070d1d189359dd70ebdca830d7ee89c2f4cbfeddafa3508f077aaaa3dbe9f059.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=2,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=5.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[5 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(10\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[10 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(10\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4d58976-470f-568c-8369-f19248e062bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4d58976-470f-568c-8369-f19248e062bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724785} :answer-field/id #uuid "86aa5092-22b9-5d33-8422-c5c7abb881f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b490136-ec2f-5da1-b206-ac3f8fdeef58" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^3-2t,\\qquad y=2t^2+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First find the point by substituting $t=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(-1)&=(-1)^3-2(-1)=1,\\\\\ny(-1)&=2(-1)^2+(-1)=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3t^2-2,\\qquad y'(t)=4t+1.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{4t+1}{3t^2-2}$. At $t=-1$,\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{4(-1)+1}{3(-1)^2-2}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(1,1)$ and slope $-3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=-3(x-1),\\\\\ny&=-3x+4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEnter $-3x+4$, the expression after $y=$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138a50a7-bff8-5414-ac21-bfa62cd602b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724775} {:answer/id #uuid "4c8d5290-0c40-53d0-a694-517e8573a43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+1=2(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724779} {:answer/id #uuid "60e3e55d-f045-5e8e-aeac-3f7ca96d31ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=2(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724780} {:answer/id #uuid "ea92a440-3418-57e9-843b-2f324572512b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=4(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724787} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d4f93c-21e4-50c5-a81e-af7c66a9b44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60e3e55d-f045-5e8e-aeac-3f7ca96d31ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-1=2(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724780} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fdd831a-231f-57d6-b886-60ad3dea5b5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87c713f2-aacd-5015-af52-14b73b293474" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\qquad y=t^2-3,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe point $P=(5,1)$ corresponds to $t=2$. Calculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at $P$.\n\nUse $m=\\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}$ and the point-slope form $y-y_1=m(x-x_1)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the two coordinate functions:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nAt $t=2$, the slope is\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(2)}{x'(2)}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nThe supplied point agrees with the equations: $x(2)=5$ and $y(2)=1$. Using $(x_1,y_1)=(5,1)$ gives\n\n$$\ny-1=2(x-5).\n$$\n\nThe dashed tangent touches the curve at $P$ and has slope $2$.\n\n![Parametric curve and its tangent at P](images/70/705f566aef076dc57f19e0e92302b08daf4efc1b06124abeb244bb09257a8647.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8152c14-5b0e-5b5d-94c0-879e7c717791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8152c14-5b0e-5b5d-94c0-879e7c717791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724786} :answer-field/id #uuid "37e3d34d-2eff-5ad6-9386-0a859af40276" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da780b9-c98e-5860-8dcd-d98956e4c73b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da780b9-c98e-5860-8dcd-d98956e4c73b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724774} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fbf58d-1680-51b6-a54a-9e23ff1f3ca7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88608bc9-2baa-572c-ad07-7eff0edab225" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\ln t+1),\\qquad y=\\ln t,\\qquad t>0.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point corresponding to $t=e$. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Give exact answers.\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}}$,\\,${{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=e$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\nx(e)=e(\\ln e+1)=2e,\\qquad y(e)=\\ln e=1.\n$$\n\nUse the product rule to differentiate $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=(\\ln t+1)+t\\cdot\\frac{1}{t}=\\ln t+2,\\\\\ny'(t)&=\\frac{1}{t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $C$ is\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(e)}{x'(e)}=\\frac{1/e}{3}=\\frac{1}{3e}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent through $(2e,1)$ therefore has equation\n\n$$\ny-1=\\frac{1}{3e}(x-2e).\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-axis, $x=0$. Substitute this into the tangent equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=\\frac{1}{3e}(0-2e)=-\\frac{2}{3},\\\\\ny&=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required intersection is\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\frac{1}{3}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51a47280-bb39-5f04-b0ed-83b425f7a5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51a47280-bb39-5f04-b0ed-83b425f7a5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d4d45ee-3006-5b2f-aa2c-8d00ff52e32f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724864} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49dc971-dd18-5521-952f-6a90d9772d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a49dc971-dd18-5521-952f-6a90d9772d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724870} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a210425-43cb-52f2-b47f-41ff540490a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e4c5f2c-f094-5c27-985a-4136ac4a86b1" :question/problem "![](images/29/2992cef2d7e9f4bbb9c208dca4abcd6749bf196b4fc7b609c6afa119a705a72f.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by\n\n$$\nx=t^4+t+1,\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=-1$. The dashed line is the normal at $C$. Determine the coordinates of the point where this normal crosses the $x$-axis. Use the equations to obtain exact values.\n\nThe normal crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}}, {{field-2}}$)$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=-1$ to find $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=(-1)^4+(-1)+1=1,\\\\\ny&=(-1)^2+1=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $C=(1,2)$. Differentiate the two coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4t^3+1,\\qquad y'(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{4t^3+1}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate it at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2(-1)}{4(-1)^3+1}=\\frac{-2}{-3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the normal is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{2/3}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(1,2)$:\n\n$$\ny-2=-\\frac{3}{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-axis, $y=0$. Substitute this and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2&=-\\frac{3}{2}(x-1),\\\\\nx-1&=\\frac{4}{3},\\\\\nx&=\\frac{7}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the normal crosses the $x$-axis at $\\left(\\dfrac{7}{3},0\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09a1a935-c6b5-5e9d-be55-613f0b75678c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724861} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0713a4-f77b-50d8-aca5-fe7d39884675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724862} {:answer/id #uuid "630f1f08-99eb-5dfb-9ecb-1734387b3f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724867} {:answer/id #uuid "d2eb1aa5-cbb6-5a1b-8d9d-c3c211345f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724872} {:answer/id #uuid "e546ba27-57f8-5448-b9ec-8af6c81792e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "630f1f08-99eb-5dfb-9ecb-1734387b3f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724867} :answer-field/id #uuid "38d7ea95-488a-51df-9ec4-f2f75d247c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a515ef87-73f9-5360-a766-e73507c149b9" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2t+1,\\qquad y=2t^2+1,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is given by $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2t$. Let $C$ be the point where $t=1$. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $t=1$ into both parametric equations to find the point:\n\n$$\nx=2(1)+1=3,\\qquad y=2(1)^2+1=3.\n$$\n\nThus $C=(3,3)$. The supplied derivative gives the tangent slope at $C$:\n\n$$\nm=2(1)=2.\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so its slope is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form through $(3,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-3&=-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}+3\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58bfad5b-32b9-5924-864c-3a90dda34375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58bfad5b-32b9-5924-864c-3a90dda34375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724866} :answer-field/id #uuid "f925a96d-0cf1-5c24-b537-20d7647f90b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d83b0db7-0158-50ec-a222-919bee323c57" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\ln(t^2)+1,\\qquad y=2t^3-3t+3,\\qquad t>0.\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point where $t=1$. Find the Cartesian equation of the normal to the curve at $C$. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe normal line is $y=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First find the coordinates of $C$ by substituting $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\ln(1^2)+1=1,\\\\\ny&=2(1)^3-3(1)+3=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $C=(1,2)$. Differentiate both coordinates with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{2t}{t^2}=\\frac{2}{t},\\qquad y'(t)=6t^2-3.\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of these derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{6t^2-3}{2/t}=\\frac{t(6t^2-3)}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt $t=1$ this gives\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{6-3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTake its negative reciprocal to obtain the normal slope:\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe normal passes through $(1,2)$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-2&=-\\frac{2}{3}(x-1),\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{2}{3}+2,\\\\\ny&=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{8}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEnter $-\\dfrac{2}{3}x+\\dfrac{8}{3}$ in the blank after $y=$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71495d14-25a1-5465-b682-cdf2bacd3561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724886} {:answer/id #uuid "c943d7e8-75e0-556a-a40b-f519529159ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c943d7e8-75e0-556a-a40b-f519529159ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724894} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c8ad643-a6bb-5baf-8ed5-3b7278f1c04b" :answer-field/key "$t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f5b926b-81ed-5413-a49d-f473d083a61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724881} {:answer/id #uuid "a97616e5-05d8-5e07-aab4-55821738aad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b926b-81ed-5413-a49d-f473d083a61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724881} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0456c9-2fb4-5ae1-be3c-19a1499dbf6e" :answer-field/key "$t=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abd2e16-f98d-5cee-852d-7bc1c6c23d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724876} {:answer/id #uuid "21604440-1103-53e8-8783-4a3617d2e8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21604440-1103-53e8-8783-4a3617d2e8e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724879} :answer-field/id #uuid "83cd678b-9c48-5b43-ae44-94949cedcd53" :answer-field/key "$t=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204cb847-07f7-57e7-bbb1-fa282f6ff891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724878} {:answer/id #uuid "4df6c35c-cc70-5487-ae80-059211f9145a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4df6c35c-cc70-5487-ae80-059211f9145a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724883} :answer-field/id #uuid "f852502e-81bf-56ff-859d-c72463f42a43" :answer-field/key "$t=\\pi$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac39a0a7-3d1e-5985-ac08-1018b462d7a7" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos t+1,\\qquad y=2\\sin t-4,\\qquad 0<t<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "For a vertical tangent, we need $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t)\\ne0$. The coordinate derivatives are\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-3\\sin t,\\qquad y'(t)=2\\cos t.\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t)=0$ gives $\\sin t=0$, so the candidates on the closed interval $[0,2\\pi]$ are $0$, $\\pi$, and $2\\pi$. The problem specifies the open interval $0<t<2\\pi$, so only $t=\\pi$ remains.\n\nCheck the other derivative:\n\n$$\ny'(\\pi)=2\\cos\\pi=-2\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the tangent is vertical only at $t=\\pi$ on the specified interval. At $t=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, it is $y'(t)$ that is zero, while $x'(t)$ is nonzero; those tangents are horizontal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2b7f5f-7501-59cf-8b4e-413d4e6e707c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724880} {:answer/id #uuid "42037cff-9c64-5f72-b21b-900c9483235f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724882} {:answer/id #uuid "aec03e6e-bb40-5b6d-a5cf-d9960c98efa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724890} {:answer/id #uuid "b47727ee-f8f7-55cd-8424-b75497ac923f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724891} {:answer/id #uuid "beaa3b1a-fe44-58e2-b1c9-9605144f6380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aec03e6e-bb40-5b6d-a5cf-d9960c98efa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724890} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda898ae-cf87-535e-a393-b62f877c10d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b52577f7-1af8-5815-be69-bafabed8b71b" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3(t+2),\\qquad y=2(t-1)^2+4,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope of a tangent to a parametric curve is\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal tangent, we need $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3,\\qquad y'(t)=4(t-1).\n$$\n\nSolve for the zero of $y'(t)$:\n\n$$\n4(t-1)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=1.\n$$\n\nSince $x'(1)=3\\ne0$, the tangent is horizontal at $t=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60197b27-c4b3-54dd-9b7e-e89700bd79d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60197b27-c4b3-54dd-9b7e-e89700bd79d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724885} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1bc4a0-9a8d-5fc9-bb01-be578df5ccc0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3402def-012f-554d-9ccb-ee3ec95957ab" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^2+2}{t+1},\\qquad y=t^2+2t,\\qquad -1<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical. Give an exact answer.\n\nThe tangent to the curve is vertical at $t=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A vertical tangent requires $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$. Using the quotient rule for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{2t(t+1)-(t^2+2)}{(t+1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{t^2+2t-2}{(t+1)^2},\\\\\ny'(t)&=2t+2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $t>-1$, the denominator $(t+1)^2$ is nonzero. Hence $x'(t)=0$ exactly when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^2+2t-2&=0\\\\\n(t+1)^2&=3\\\\\nt&=-1\\pm\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe root $-1-\\sqrt{3}$ is outside the given interval. The remaining candidate is $t=\\sqrt{3}-1$. Check that its other coordinate derivative is nonzero:\n\n$$\ny'(\\sqrt{3}-1)=2(\\sqrt{3}-1)+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\ne0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is vertical at $t=\\sqrt{3}-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fac786-edb1-50f4-b7c0-4981772d6f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fac786-edb1-50f4-b7c0-4981772d6f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724962} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bc960f4-7d98-58f1-b946-a18f0fc8d04e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895ad559-f08f-5928-97ba-4ccb54f53349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "895ad559-f08f-5928-97ba-4ccb54f53349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724966} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6851b0-151c-595c-8688-823918b39e36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d6192ee-d81b-576f-9c34-2cc6df4d5f6c" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t^2+3}{t-1},\\qquad y=t^2+1,\\qquad -2<t<4,\\quad t\\ne1.\n$$\n\nDetermine all values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers in **ascending order**.\n\nThe normal is horizontal at $t=$ {{field-1}} and $t=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal when the tangent is vertical. We therefore need $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$ at parameters in the given domain.\n\nUsing the quotient rule for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{2t(t-1)-(t^2+3)}{(t-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{t^2-2t-3}{(t-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(t-3)(t+1)}{(t-1)^2},\\\\\ny'(t)&=2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $t\\ne1$, the denominator of $x'(t)$ is nonzero. Hence $x'(t)=0$ exactly when\n\n$$\n(t-3)(t+1)=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=-1\\ \\text{or}\\ t=3.\n$$\n\nBoth values lie in $-2<t<4$ and neither equals the excluded parameter $1$. The value $t=1$ is not a candidate: the original curve is undefined there.\n\nCheck the other derivative:\n\n$$\ny'(-1)=-2\\ne0,\\qquad y'(3)=6\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the normal is horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=3$, in that order. Although $y'(0)=0$, we have $x'(0)=-3\\ne0$, so $t=0$ gives a horizontal tangent and a vertical normal instead."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211d97f3-d99d-58c2-8fd5-7b6fd4b3e35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724957} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfec1b5-6cfa-57ef-ac45-2751184da881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724963} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcff6a0-d5b5-538d-a8d2-70089ddf0fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724965} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52cf59-ee06-539c-a1f5-732093fa9e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724968} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bb8da7-dae2-53e8-b803-83cb53948be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a52cf59-ee06-539c-a1f5-732093fa9e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724968} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ad0f3a-3bb6-5f20-9659-e6f10d2e32de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7309ec21-e629-5015-953e-195cabe13f77" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t,\\qquad y=t^3+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal when the tangent is vertical. We therefore need $x'(t)=0$ and $y'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2t-6,\\qquad y'(t)=3t^2+1.\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t-6=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=3.\n$$\n\nAt this parameter,\n\n$$\ny'(3)=3(3)^2+1=28\\ne0.\n$$\n\nThus the tangent is vertical and the normal is horizontal at $t=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f67c4a5-db13-5858-8707-67f41c1afede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f67c4a5-db13-5858-8707-67f41c1afede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724960} :answer-field/id #uuid "953bb1a2-99c6-5a30-a4c3-2778c7445a26" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406f776a-dbcd-5466-9bfb-6c12dec0604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "406f776a-dbcd-5466-9bfb-6c12dec0604a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724961} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4d186d-2e39-5a2e-968e-45d3746a4252" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186724972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ebce9dd-f1fe-5297-b3dc-3c91b5f33ad0" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)+2,\\\\\ny&=2\\sin\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)-1,\\\\\n&\\hspace{1em}0\\le t<2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDetermine all values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in **ascending order**.\n\nThe normal is vertical at $t=$ {{field-1}} and $t=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The normal is vertical when the tangent is horizontal. We therefore need $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$.\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=-3\\sin\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right),\\\\\ny'(t)&=2\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $y'(t)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos\\left(t-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)&=0,\\\\\nt-\\frac{\\pi}{6}&=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi,\\\\\nt&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}+k\\pi,\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly $k=0$ and $k=1$ place $t$ in $0\\le t<2\\pi$, giving $t=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$. Check the other derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)&=-3\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=-3\\ne0,\\\\\nx'\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right)&=-3\\sin\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)=3\\ne0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is vertical at $t=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{3}$, in that order."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da907729-4c1d-5015-a11f-86df99198cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2} x + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da907729-4c1d-5015-a11f-86df99198cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 \\sqrt{2} x + 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725135} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2394000-3072-5997-98c2-8db79b648a42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2246cdba-960e-5adf-9ba9-50ed01a60c42" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\sqrt{t + 1} \\\\ y&=- t^{3} - 3 \\\\ t>-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=\\sqrt{\\left(1\\right) + 1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ y_0&=- \\left(1\\right)^{3} - 3 \\\\ &=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t + 1}} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 3 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(1)}{x\\prime(1)}&=\\frac{-3}{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}}=- 6 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(\\sqrt{2},-4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+4&=- 6 \\sqrt{2}\\left(x-\\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ y&=- 6 \\sqrt{2} x + 12+\\left(-4\\right) \\\\ y&=- 6 \\sqrt{2} x + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acb842a-188d-5847-9a84-0e4b9f5d1305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1acb842a-188d-5847-9a84-0e4b9f5d1305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 x - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725123} :answer-field/id #uuid "709fbcc9-920f-593b-83be-2ae844ed3800" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d63b761-e5de-5dcc-a2db-b53ba454c6a4" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{5} - 3 t^{3} \\\\ y&=- 2 t^{2} \\\\ -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=2 \\left(-1\\right)^{5} - 3 \\left(-1\\right)^{3} \\\\ &=1 \\\\ y_0&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=10 t^{4} - 9 t^{2} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 4 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(-1)}{x\\prime(-1)}&=\\frac{4}{1}=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(1,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+2&=4\\left(x-1\\right) \\\\ y&=4 x - 4+\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ y&=4 x - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d21a65b6-c9ad-539a-b56d-852bc777055d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 x}{7} - \\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21a65b6-c9ad-539a-b56d-852bc777055d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2 x}{7} - \\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725133} :answer-field/id #uuid "009c2dbe-54ce-53b8-bc6a-2c652ad1dc2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44cf6bdd-2913-5701-9476-5a889a47312c" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=2 t^{5} - t^{3} \\\\ y&=- t^{2} \\\\ -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=2 \\left(1\\right)^{5} - \\left(1\\right)^{3} \\\\ &=1 \\\\ y_0&=- \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=10 t^{4} - 3 t^{2} \\\\ y'(t)&=- 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(1)}{x\\prime(1)}&=\\frac{-2}{7}=- \\frac{2}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(1,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny+1&=- \\frac{2}{7}\\left(x-1\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{2 x}{7} + \\frac{2}{7}+\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{2 x}{7} - \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308d1ace-0fbe-53eb-bc1c-d077343fd2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308d1ace-0fbe-53eb-bc1c-d077343fd2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725125} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f79add-48ac-54f5-bf8f-bd66e9230937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f7641c8-52ba-5b03-b24d-5ccb9db990d2" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t \\\\ y&=- t^{5} \\\\ -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=-4 \\\\ y_0&=- \\left(-1\\right)^{5} \\\\ &=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=- 4 t + 2 \\\\ y'(t)&=- 5 t^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(-1)}{x\\prime(-1)}&=\\frac{-5}{6}=- \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(-4,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-1&=- \\frac{5}{6}\\left(x+4\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{10}{3}+\\left(1\\right) \\\\ y&=- \\frac{5 x}{6} - \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09432941-02e7-5a60-a9fa-7a1aa6c583b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 x - 65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09432941-02e7-5a60-a9fa-7a1aa6c583b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 x - 65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725122} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d15edd1-fd1a-500a-adea-2b225ceafff6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da55a9f2-313f-5cb2-b8c0-cbdc5f9388d0" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\sqrt{t + 3} \\\\ y&=3 t^{3} + 4 \\\\ t>-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1$.\n\nThe equation of the tangent line is $y=$ {{field-1}}. Give an exact expression." :question/worked-solution "First evaluate the coordinates at $t=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_0&=\\sqrt{\\left(1\\right) + 3} \\\\ &=2 \\\\ y_0&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{3} + 4 \\\\ &=7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t + 3}} \\\\ y'(t)&=9 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope is the ratio of the coordinate derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=\\frac{y\\prime(1)}{x\\prime(1)}&=\\frac{9}{\\frac{1}{4}}=36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse point-slope form with $(2,7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-7&=36\\left(x-2\\right) \\\\ y&=36 x - 72+\\left(7\\right) \\\\ y&=36 x - 65\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080819a3-8281-5402-ab17-f69e3dc6617d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725218} {:answer/id #uuid "2f917ccd-f497-57a2-b611-ddc131c37da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725223} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0cee05-8b17-56af-bf15-0429e66f1ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725240} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cad4c2-bfd1-5137-bcbc-046a2294d0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725242} {:answer/id #uuid "edd2c90c-bf3e-5a9d-b063-354b2b15ab0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd2c90c-bf3e-5a9d-b063-354b2b15ab0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725249} :answer-field/id #uuid "473d2738-4793-5d73-9716-79a0f2be2c6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "112bfd7e-5ac8-5643-9292-9a6f8609d7a0" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x&=2 t - 4 \\\\ y&=\\left(t + 3\\right)^{3} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers." :question/worked-solution "The parametric slope is $m=y'(t)/x'(t)$. A horizontal tangent requires $y'(t)=0$ and $x'(t)\\ne0$. Differentiate both coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(t)&=2 \\\\ y'(t)&=3 \\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolve $y'(t)=0$ and retain precisely the values in the stated domain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 \\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}&=0 \\\\ t&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck the other derivative at each surviving value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3)&=2\\ne0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent is horizontal at $t=-3$."}]}
